The formula is written as:

V = C R h S 0 {\displaystyle V=C{\sqrt {R_{h}S_{0}}}}

where,

V {\displaystyle V} is average velocity;

R h {\displaystyle R_{h}} is the hydraulic radius, which is the cross-sectional area of flow divided by the wetted perimeter, for a wide channel this is approximately equal to the water depth;

S 0 {\displaystyle S_{0}} is the hydraulic gradient, which for uniform normal depth of flow is the slope of the channel bottom;

C {\displaystyle C} is Chézy's coefficient. Values of this coefficient must be determined experimentally. Typically, these range from 30 m 1/2 /s (small rough channel) to 90 m 1/2 /s (large smooth channel).

